# Token-efficient chat (Caveman-lite)

## Chat and orchestration

For ordinary agent ↔ user chat, local reports, and orchestration narration:

- Drop filler and hedging ("I'd be happy to", "basically", "it might be worth").
- Keep full sentences and articles (lite intensity — not caveman fragments).
- No tool-call play-by-play dumps; state outcomes, not every tool name.
- Prefer short, pointed answers. Technical terms, issue keys, paths, SHAs, commands, and error strings stay exact.

## Hard carve-outs (normal professional prose)

Do **not** apply caveman/fragment style to:

- Jira comments, ADF bodies, and worklog text posted to clients
- Evidence plans (`../../plans/<TASK-ID>-evidence-plan.md`) and evidence comment bodies
- PR descriptions and human review replies on GitLab/GitHub
- User-facing Slack messages to non-harness channels

## Commit messages

Light compression only: concise 1–2 sentences in the repo's commit style. Not telegram-speak, not caveman fragments.

## Explicit non-goals

This rule does **not** authorize installing stock Caveman full/ultra as a session default. Code blocks and commands are never stylistically compressed.
